			<div class="content" id="post-content-2541818">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Click&nbsp;this&nbsp;for&nbsp;line&nbsp;by&nbsp;line&nbsp;critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>passerine aviary</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Over half of all birds are passerine, so I'm not sure this specificity is necessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>an insect diet consistent with that of members of the species Mimus polyglottos(northern mockingbird)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Mockingbirds are omnivorous, so they eat berries, seeds, fruit, many other small animals (both invertebrates and vertebrates) and even tree sap. A mockingbird fed just insects would likely be pretty malnourished.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>are genetically mockingbirds</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So are they &quot;genetically amorphous&quot; or genetically mockingbirds?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2619-1 will instead turn into the subject's least preferred species of bird and become hostile</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does that make sense? Presumably the bird is trying to get in good with the human, right? Why not turn into like a Little Penguin, or kakapo, or something equally non-threatening?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2619-1 contains much higher quantities of anomalous DNA than other instances of SCP-2619, with approximately 105 chromosomes, more than 5 times more than a standard mockingbird</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That is not how DNA works. At all.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>5 chromosomes containing an unknown form of prokaryotic DNA</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Prokaryotes almost exclusively have only one chromosome, and maybe a few plasmids.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>chromosomes containing triple-helix nucleotide chains</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is very plainly not DNA then.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These chains have since been identified as trioxyribonucleic acid</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No, they wouldn't be. The &quot;Deoxy&quot; in DNA refers to the lack of an oxygen on the 5-carbon sugar used to construct the molecule, not how many strands are in the helix.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A black phoebe, //sayornis nigricans/</p>
</blockquote>
<p>By the time you read this, I will have corrected it for you, but the genus name of a species should always be capitalized.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>were found to have pure mockingbird DNA in 75% of all cells</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, not really how DNA works.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>suggesting the birds were actually mockingbirds genetically altered to appear as different species</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No, that would suggest that they were maybe close relatives, or some kind of freak hybrid. A 25% genetic difference is pretty big.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
So this article is pretty well written, from a technical standpoint, but the actual object is sort of bland. It's a bird that transforms into other birds, and that's about it. There are some hints at backstory throughout the article, but I don't feel that you develop it enough to keep a reader interested.
<p>Also: You seem to have a few misunderstandings as to how DNA and genetics actually work.</p>

							</div>

								
					
