<p>I don't know about it absolutely not working, if you used totally different bases (with more 3-D stereochemistry than our relatively flat bases) that could form trimers by hydrogen bonds I think there are probably theoretically possible geometries. It wouldn't have higher density as an information storage molecule, probably, because there would still be only one sense strand, although it might have better error correction (though it might just have more errors due to complexity; this is an information theory problem that I doubt is trivial to solve). It won't however be directly compatible with our protein synthesis mechanisms, although obviously codons are totally arbitrary and it could produce the same polypeptides as end products.</p>

